artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a forest divided into a finite set of management units (stands) $S = \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$ on a planar landscape, with an adjacency graph $G = (S, E)$ encoding which stands share a common boundary. A harvest schedule over a planning horizon $T = \{1, \dots, |T|\}$ assigns to each stand a decision variable indicating whether (and when) it is harvested. Two classical spatial harvest scheduling formulations are:

(a) The Unit Restriction Model (URM): each stand is itself a management unit, and no two adjacent stands may be harvested within the same "green-up" period. Formally, with binary variables $x_{i,t} \in \{0,1\}$ for stand $i$ harvested in period $t$, one imposes $x_{i,t} + x_{j,t'} \le 1$ for all $(i,j) \in E$ and $|t - t'| < g$, where $g$ is the green-up length.

(b) The Area Restriction Model (ARM): contiguous clusters of stands may be harvested simultaneously provided the total harvested area of any such cluster does not exceed a prescribed maximum opening size $A_{\max}$.

Both models seek to maximize the net present value of timber harvested subject to these spatial constraints (plus standard flow, inventory, and sustainability constraints).

Devise extensions of the URM and ARM mixed-integer programming formulations that additionally enforce richer environmental considerations, including at least:
(i) bounds on landscape fragmentation (e.g., on the number, total perimeter, or perimeter-to-area ratios of harvest openings or of mature-forest patches);
(ii) maintenance of wildlife corridors, i.e., guaranteed connected paths through mature forest between designated landscape regions;
(iii) control of edge effects between mature and harvested patches;
(iv) preservation of a balanced spatial distribution of mature forest patches of prescribed minimum sizes throughout the horizon.

The problem is to construct such extended formulations and accompanying solution methods so that realistic-scale instances (hundreds to thousands of stands and many time periods) can still be solved to provable optimality, or to within a small optimality gap, in practically acceptable computation time. In particular, identify a formulation that simultaneously incorporates constraints of types (i)–(iv) and admits a tractable exact or approximate algorithm with theoretical performance guarantees.
